[Music]  we used to go fishing for a day and we'd  catch enough fish to sell at the market  and nowadays we're lucky if we can bring  in one or two how can I call myself a  fisherman if there's no fish to catch  you know it's clear that the stock is  depleting and it's mainly because of  these vessels  industrial vessels that you see around  me really think of the Seychelles you  probably think of some sand beautiful  beaches and turquoise waters before my  mentor talk to you about isn't just this  we're looking at overfishing across the  world and here in the Seychelles the  local fishermen say it's all about a  battle between David and Goliath the big  industrial fishing trawler to become  here essentially dredged any tuna they  want out of these waters  you get sense of the scale of what we're  talking about here at the port now  consider the Heath's boat could take  maybe 5 10 maybe 15 tonnes of tuna if  he's lucky and that this one can take  about 2,000 tons of tuna and it's  constantly unloading the stuff it goes  from here off to the tuna cannery one of  the biggest in the world producing about  1.7 million cans a day you might have  noticed this is actually a European ship  it's from Spain from the Basque Country  they've got the right to fish in these  waters now they say that's not a problem  there's enough fish for everyone but the  local fishermen say I've got a bit of a  problem  things have changed around here  it isn't making all these in my father's  time  there were plenty of fish to go around  then fishing is our livelihood and I've  fished these waters to provide for my  family I just couldn't stand by anymore  and watch what was happening as humans  we all have to do our bit to change this  [Music]

